arm_mptimer, a9gtimer: tick at the Zynq-7000's 333 MHz PERIPHCLK

QEMU's Cortex-A9 private and global timers count at 100 MHz. On the
Zynq-7000 PERIPHCLK is CPU_3x2x = 333.33 MHz, and VEXos relies on it: both
CPUs load the private timer with 0x51615 (333,333) for a 1 ms tick. At
100 MHz every "millisecond" lasted 3.33 ms, so vexSystemTimeGet, task
sleeps and the CPU0 main-loop pacing all ran 3.3x slow.

This tree is only used for the vex-v5 machine, so the period is changed
outright rather than made a property.

diff --git a/hw/timer/a9gtimer.c b/hw/timer/a9gtimer.c
index 3a08691..d3de0fa 100644
--- a/hw/timer/a9gtimer.c
+++ b/hw/timer/a9gtimer.c
@@ -65,7 +65,7 @@ static inline uint64_t a9_gtimer_get_conv(A9GTimerState *s)
     uint64_t prescale = extract32(s->control, R_CONTROL_PRESCALER_SHIFT,
                                   R_CONTROL_PRESCALER_LEN);
 
-    return (prescale + 1) * 10;
+    return (prescale + 1) * 3;
 }
 
 static A9GTimerUpdate a9_gtimer_get_update(A9GTimerState *s)
diff --git a/hw/timer/arm_mptimer.c b/hw/timer/arm_mptimer.c
index 8815814..d6b6457 100644
--- a/hw/timer/arm_mptimer.c
+++ b/hw/timer/arm_mptimer.c
@@ -61,7 +61,7 @@ static inline void timerblock_update_irq(TimerBlock *tb)
 /* Return conversion factor from mpcore timer ticks to qemu timer ticks.  */
 static inline uint32_t timerblock_scale(uint32_t control)
 {
-    return (((control >> 8) & 0xff) + 1) * 10;
+    return (((control >> 8) & 0xff) + 1) * 3;
 }
 
 /* Must be called within a ptimer transaction block */
